raw=json.loads("{\"request\":{\"url\":\"https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/\",\"formats\":[\"markdown\"],\"onlyMainContent\":true,\"maxAge\":0,\"timeout\":30000,\"domainTools\":false},\"response\":{\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"{\\n  \\\"markdown\\\": \\\"[Cem Yuksel](https://www.cemyuksel.com/) ![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/rainbow_heart.svg)\\\\n\\\\n[Research](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research)\\\\n\\\\n[Contact](https://www.cemyuksel.com/contact) [Resume](https://www.cemyuksel.com/resume) [Research](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research) [Teaching](https://www.cemyuksel.com/courses) [Students](https://www.cemyuksel.com/students) [Software](https://www.cemyuksel.com/software) [Animation](https://www.cemyuksel.com/animation) [Music](https://www.cemyuksel.com/music)dark-mode: off\\\\n\\\\n# Stitch Meshes\\\\n\\\\n[Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/) [Jonathan M. Kaldor](http://www.graphics.cornell.edu/~jmkaldor/) [Doug L. James](http://www.cs.cornell.edu/~djames/) [Steve Marschner](http://www.cs.cornell.edu/~srm/)\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/sheep_small2.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/sheep.jpg)\\\\n\\\\nA knitted sweater dress for a sheep with Ridged Feather pattern, prepared using our knitted garment modeling system.\\\\nThe sheep character and the input mesh of the garment is modeled by [Rune Spaans](http://www.superrune.com/).\\\\n\\\\n## Abstract\\\\n\\\\nRecent yarn-based simulation techniques permit realistic and efficient dynamic\\\\nsimulation of knitted clothing, but producing the required yarn-level models remains a\\\\nchallenge. The lack of practical modeling techniques significantly limits the diversity\\\\nand complexity of knitted garments that can be simulated. We propose a new modeling\\\\ntechnique that builds yarn-level models of complex knitted garments for virtual\\\\ncharacters. We start with a polygonal model that represents the large-scale surface of\\\\nthe knitted cloth. Using this mesh as an input, our interactive modeling tool produces a\\\\nfiner mesh representing the layout of stitches in the garment, which we call the\\\\n_stitch mesh_. By manipulating this mesh and assigning stitch types to its faces,\\\\nthe user can replicate a variety of complicated knitting patterns. The curve model\\\\nrepresenting the yarn is generated from the stitch mesh, then the final shape is\\\\ncomputed by a yarn-level physical simulation that locally relaxes the yarn into\\\\nrealistic shape while preserving global shape of the garment and avoiding \\\\\\\\`\\\\\\\\`yarn\\\\npull-through,'' thereby producing valid yarn geometry suitable for dynamic\\\\nsimulation. Using our system, we can efficiently create yarn-level models of knitted\\\\nclothing with a rich variety of patterns that would be completely impractical to model\\\\nusing traditional techniques. We show a variety of example knitting patterns and\\\\nfull-scale garments produced using our system.\\\\n\\\\nSee also\\\\n[the Project Page at Cornell University](http://www.cs.cornell.edu/projects/stitchmeshes/).\\\\n\\\\n## Images\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/overview_small.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/overview.jpg)\\\\n\\\\n**Stages of our knitted garment modeling**\\\\n**system:** (a) We begin our interactive modeling process with a\\\\npolygonal mesh that specifies the global shape of the cloth model;\\\\n(b) using this polygonal mesh we produce a high-resolution\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\emph{stitch mesh} that serves as a canvas-like abstraction of the\\\\nyarn model; (c) then, we specify the desired knitting pattern over\\\\nthe stitch mesh's surface. (d) Following the interactive modeling\\\\nprocess, the model goes through offline relaxation, beginning with a\\\\nmesh-based relaxation that moves the stitch mesh to the subdivision\\\\nsurface of the input model and slides its vertices over this surface\\\\nbased on the topology of the knitting pattern; finally, (e) we\\\\ngenerate the yarn curves and (f) use a physically based relaxation\\\\nprocess at the yarn level to compute the final realistic shape.\\\\nThe input mesh is modeled by [Alexander Tomchuk](http://alexandert.cgsociety.org/).\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/dress1_small.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/dress1.jpg)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/dress2_small.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/dress2.jpg)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/poncho_small2.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/poncho.jpg)\\\\n\\\\nKnitted dresses with two different stitch mesh layouts and knitting patterns, and knitted poncho with a Ribbing pattern, simulated on a mannequin.\\\\nThe mannequin and the input mesh of the dress is modeled by [Alexander Tomchuk](http://alexandert.cgsociety.org/).\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/glove_small2.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/glove.jpg)\\\\n\\\\nKnitted glove with a Ribbing pattern formed by alternating knit and purl stitches.\\\\nThe hand model and the input mesh is modeled by [Alexander Tomchuk](http://alexandert.cgsociety.org/).\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/teacozy_small2.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/teacozy.jpg)\\\\n\\\\nKnitted tea cozy with a Stockinette pattern formed by repeated knit stitches.\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/alien_small.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/alien.jpg)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/alien-cables_small.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/alien-cables.jpg)\\\\n\\\\nA knitted sweater for an alien character and the same sweater model with the Braid Cables pattern.\\\\nThe alien character is modeled by [Christer Sveen](http://www.christersveen.com/) and the input mesh of the sweater is modeled by [Rune Spaans](http://www.superrune.com/).\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/patterns_small.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/patterns.jpg)\\\\n\\\\n**Comparisons to real knitted samples.** The images at the top are photos of real-world knitted samples\\\\nand the bottom ones are the results of our simulation. Considering that the results of knitting a particular\\\\npattern depend on the yarn and needles used, as well as the style\\\\nof the individual knitter, these images show that we can qualitatively\\\\nmatch the visual appearance of various patterns. Many thanks to\\\\n[Deborah Dawson](http://www.cayugaartisans.com/) for knitting the patterns 1, 2, 3, and 5, and\\\\n[Schoolhouse Press](http://www.schoolhousepress.com/)\\\\nfor granting us permission to use two photos (patterns 4 and 6)\\\\nfrom [A Fourth Treasury of Knitting Patterns by Barbara G. Walker](http://www.amazon.com/A-Fourth-Treasury-Knitting-Patterns/dp/0942018206).\\\\n\\\\n## Videos\\\\n\\\\nStitch Meshes for Modeling Knitted Clothing with Yarn-level Detail (SIGGRAPH 2012) - YouTube\\\\n\\\\nTap to unmute\\\\n\\\\n[Stitch Meshes for Modeling Knitted Clothing with Yarn-level Detail (SIGGRAPH 2012)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NG5C_a6rxrY) [Cem Yuksel](https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCfGIWnnZY79KO0b12O57byw)\\\\n\\\\nCem Yuksel23.6K subscribers\\\\n\\\\n[Watch on](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NG5C_a6rxrY)\\\\n\\\\nStitch Meshes for Modeling Knitted Clothing with Yarn-level Detail, SIGGRAPH 2012 paper video.\\\\n\\\\n## Publications\\\\n\\\\n[Yura Hwang](http://yurahwang.com/), [Jenny Han Lin](https://jlin98.github.io/), [Jerry Hsu](https://chichenghsu.com/), [Benjamin Mastripolito](https://www.linkedin.com/in/benjamin-mastripolito-291a1316a/), [Jim McCann](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~jmccann/), [Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/)[Neighbor-Aware Data-Driven Relaxation of Stitch Mesh Models for Knits](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/neighbor-aware-stitch-mesh-relaxation.pdf)In SIGGRAPH Asia 2025 Conference Papers\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/neighbor-aware-stitch-mesh-relaxation-paper.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/neighbor-aware-stitch-mesh-relaxation.pdf)\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-pdf.svg)Paper](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/neighbor-aware-stitch-mesh-relaxation.pdf) \\\\\\\\- 41.3MB[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-library.svg)Publisher's Page](https://doi.org/10.1145/3757377.3763890)![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-bibtex.svg)BibTeX\\\\n\\\\n[Kui Wu](https://kuiwuchn.github.io/), [Marco Tarini](http://vcg.isti.cnr.it/~tarini/), [Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/), [Jim McCann](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~jmccann/), [Xifeng Gao](https://gaoxifeng.github.io/)[Wearable 3D Machine Knitting](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/wearable.pdf)IEEE Transactions on Visualization and Computer Graphics, 2021\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/wearable_tvcg2021.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/wearable.pdf)\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-pdf.svg)Paper (Preprint)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/wearable.pdf) \\\\\\\\- 8.0MB[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-library.svg)Publisher's Page](http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/TVCG.2021.3056101)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-video.svg)Youtube Video](http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PAZYmuYK8IE)![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-bibtex.svg)BibTeX\\\\n\\\\n[Vidya Narayanan\\\\\\\\*](http://www.cs.cmu.edu/~nvidya/), [Kui Wu\\\\\\\\*](https://kuiwuchn.github.io/), [Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/), [Jim McCann](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~jmccann/)[Visual Knitting Machine Programming](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/visualknitting.pdf)ACM Transactions on Graphics (SIGGRAPH 2019), 38, 4, 2019(\\\\\\\\*Joint First Authors)\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/visualknitting.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/visualknitting.pdf)\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-pdf.svg)Paper](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/visualknitting.pdf) \\\\\\\\- 18.8MB[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-library.svg)Publisher's Page](http://doi.acm.org/10.1145/3306346.3322995)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-video.svg)Youtube Video](http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9ms5uEUC_js)![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-bibtex.svg)BibTeX\\\\n\\\\n[Kui Wu](https://kuiwuchn.github.io/), [Hannah Swan](https://www.linkedin.com/in/hannahswan/), [Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/)[Knittable Stitch Meshes](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/knittablestitchmeshes.pdf)ACM Transactions on Graphics, 38, 1, 2019\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/knittablestitchmeshes.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/knittablestitchmeshes.pdf)\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-pdf.svg)Paper](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/knittablestitchmeshes.pdf) \\\\\\\\- 48.4MB[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-library.svg)Publisher's Page](http://doi.acm.org/10.1145/3292481)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-video.svg)Youtube Video](http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9jcoREh70Bg)![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-bibtex.svg)BibTeX\\\\n\\\\n[Kui Wu](https://kuiwuchn.github.io/), [Xifeng Gao](https://gaoxifeng.github.io/), [Zachary Ferguson](http://zfergus.me/), [Daniele Panozzo](https://cims.nyu.edu/gcl/daniele.html), [Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/)[Stitch Meshing](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshing.pdf)ACM Transactions on Graphics (SIGGRAPH 2018), 37, 4, 2018\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshing.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshing.pdf)\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-pdf.svg)Paper](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshing.pdf) \\\\\\\\- 75.7MB[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-library.svg)Publisher's Page](http://doi.acm.org/10.1145/3197517.3201360)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-video.svg)Youtube Video](http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FuVPUS4b_5g)![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-bibtex.svg)BibTeX\\\\n\\\\n[Kui Wu](https://kuiwuchn.github.io/), [Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/)3D Modeling of Knittable Structures with Stitch MeshesIn Proceedings of the Fiber Society 2016 Technical Conference\\\\n\\\\n![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/papers/paper.jpg)\\\\n\\\\n![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-bibtex.svg)BibTeX\\\\n\\\\n[Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/), [Jonathan M. Kaldor](http://www.graphics.cornell.edu/~jmkaldor/), [Doug L. James](http://www.cs.cornell.edu/~djames/), [Steve Marschner](http://www.cs.cornell.edu/~srm/)[Stitch Meshes for Modeling Knitted Clothing with Yarn-Level Detail](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshes.pdf)ACM Transactions on Graphics (SIGGRAPH 2012), 31, 3, 2012\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshes.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshes.pdf)\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-pdf.svg)Paper (Preprint)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshes.pdf) \\\\\\\\- 9.1MB[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-pdf.svg)Paper (Preprint, High Resolution)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/stitchmeshes-highres.pdf) \\\\\\\\- 22.1MB[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-library.svg)Publisher's Page](http://doi.acm.org/10.1145/2185520.2185533)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-video.svg)Youtube Video](http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NG5C_a6rxrY)![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-bibtex.svg)BibTeX© ACM, 2012. This is the author's version of the work. It is posted here by permission of ACM for your personal use. Not for redistribution. The definitive version was published in ACM Transactions on Graphics (SIGGRAPH 2012), {31, 3, August 2012}\\\\n\\\\n## Related Links\\\\n\\\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/mitsuba.jpg)](http://www.mitsuba-renderer.org/)\\\\n\\\\nAll images used in the project were rendered using [Mitsuba](http://www.mitsuba-renderer.org/).\\\\n\\\\nloading\\\",\\n  \\\"metadata\\\": {\\n    \\\"ogTitle\\\": \\\"Stitch Meshes - Cem Yuksel\\\",\\n    \\\"viewport\\\": [\\n      \\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\",\\n      \\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"language\\\": \\\"en\\\",\\n    \\\"og:title\\\": \\\"Stitch Meshes - Cem Yuksel\\\",\\n    \\\"title\\\": \\\"Stitch Meshes - Cem Yuksel\\\",\\n    \\\"favicon\\\": \\\"http://www.cemyuksel.com/cem.ico\\\",\\n    \\\"scrapeId\\\": \\\"01a156ee-bad4-731b-8a75-f785516226b6\\\",\\n    \\\"sourceURL\\\": \\\"https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/\\\",\\n    \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/\\\",\\n    \\\"statusCode\\\": 200,\\n    \\\"contentType\\\": \\\"text/html; charset=UTF-8\\\",\\n    \\\"timezone\\\": \\\"America/New_York\\\",\\n    \\\"proxyUsed\\\": \\\"basic\\\",\\n    \\\"indexId\\\": \\\"b5f26e2d-ccb8-46d4-9975-a27d77e31d35\\\",\\n    \\\"creditsUsed\\\": 1,\\n    \\\"concurrencyLimited\\\": false\\n  }\\n}\"}],\"structuredContent\":{\"markdown\":\"[Cem Yuksel](https://www.cemyuksel.com/) ![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/rainbow_heart.svg)\\n\\n[Research](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research)\\n\\n[Contact](https://www.cemyuksel.com/contact) [Resume](https://www.cemyuksel.com/resume) [Research](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research) [Teaching](https://www.cemyuksel.com/courses) [Students](https://www.cemyuksel.com/students) [Software](https://www.cemyuksel.com/software) [Animation](https://www.cemyuksel.com/animation) [Music](https://www.cemyuksel.com/music)dark-mode: off\\n\\n# Stitch Meshes\\n\\n[Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/) [Jonathan M. Kaldor](http://www.graphics.cornell.edu/~jmkaldor/) [Doug L. James](http://www.cs.cornell.edu/~djames/) [Steve Marschner](http://www.cs.cornell.edu/~srm/)\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/sheep_small2.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/sheep.jpg)\\n\\nA knitted sweater dress for a sheep with Ridged Feather pattern, prepared using our knitted garment modeling system.\\nThe sheep character and the input mesh of the garment is modeled by [Rune Spaans](http://www.superrune.com/).\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nRecent yarn-based simulation techniques permit realistic and efficient dynamic\\nsimulation of knitted clothing, but producing the required yarn-level models remains a\\nchallenge. The lack of practical modeling techniques significantly limits the diversity\\nand complexity of knitted garments that can be simulated. We propose a new modeling\\ntechnique that builds yarn-level models of complex knitted garments for virtual\\ncharacters. We start with a polygonal model that represents the large-scale surface of\\nthe knitted cloth. Using this mesh as an input, our interactive modeling tool produces a\\nfiner mesh representing the layout of stitches in the garment, which we call the\\n_stitch mesh_. By manipulating this mesh and assigning stitch types to its faces,\\nthe user can replicate a variety of complicated knitting patterns. The curve model\\nrepresenting the yarn is generated from the stitch mesh, then the final shape is\\ncomputed by a yarn-level physical simulation that locally relaxes the yarn into\\nrealistic shape while preserving global shape of the garment and avoiding \\\\`\\\\`yarn\\npull-through,'' thereby producing valid yarn geometry suitable for dynamic\\nsimulation. Using our system, we can efficiently create yarn-level models of knitted\\nclothing with a rich variety of patterns that would be completely impractical to model\\nusing traditional techniques. We show a variety of example knitting patterns and\\nfull-scale garments produced using our system.\\n\\nSee also\\n[the Project Page at Cornell University](http://www.cs.cornell.edu/projects/stitchmeshes/).\\n\\n## Images\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/overview_small.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/overview.jpg)\\n\\n**Stages of our knitted garment modeling**\\n**system:** (a) We begin our interactive modeling process with a\\npolygonal mesh that specifies the global shape of the cloth model;\\n(b) using this polygonal mesh we produce a high-resolution\\n\\\\\\\\emph{stitch mesh} that serves as a canvas-like abstraction of the\\nyarn model; (c) then, we specify the desired knitting pattern over\\nthe stitch mesh's surface. (d) Following the interactive modeling\\nprocess, the model goes through offline relaxation, beginning with a\\nmesh-based relaxation that moves the stitch mesh to the subdivision\\nsurface of the input model and slides its vertices over this surface\\nbased on the topology of the knitting pattern; finally, (e) we\\ngenerate the yarn curves and (f) use a physically based relaxation\\nprocess at the yarn level to compute the final realistic shape.\\nThe input mesh is modeled by [Alexander Tomchuk](http://alexandert.cgsociety.org/).\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/dress1_small.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/dress1.jpg)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/dress2_small.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/dress2.jpg)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/poncho_small2.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/poncho.jpg)\\n\\nKnitted dresses with two different stitch mesh layouts and knitting patterns, and knitted poncho with a Ribbing pattern, simulated on a mannequin.\\nThe mannequin and the input mesh of the dress is modeled by [Alexander Tomchuk](http://alexandert.cgsociety.org/).\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/glove_small2.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/glove.jpg)\\n\\nKnitted glove with a Ribbing pattern formed by alternating knit and purl stitches.\\nThe hand model and the input mesh is modeled by [Alexander Tomchuk](http://alexandert.cgsociety.org/).\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/teacozy_small2.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/teacozy.jpg)\\n\\nKnitted tea cozy with a Stockinette pattern formed by repeated knit stitches.\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/alien_small.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/alien.jpg)[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/alien-cables_small.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/alien-cables.jpg)\\n\\nA knitted sweater for an alien character and the same sweater model with the Braid Cables pattern.\\nThe alien character is modeled by [Christer Sveen](http://www.christersveen.com/) and the input mesh of the sweater is modeled by [Rune Spaans](http://www.superrune.com/).\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/patterns_small.png)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/patterns.jpg)\\n\\n**Comparisons to real knitted samples.** The images at the top are photos of real-world knitted samples\\nand the bottom ones are the results of our simulation. Considering that the results of knitting a particular\\npattern depend on the yarn and needles used, as well as the style\\nof the individual knitter, these images show that we can qualitatively\\nmatch the visual appearance of various patterns. Many thanks to\\n[Deborah Dawson](http://www.cayugaartisans.com/) for knitting the patterns 1, 2, 3, and 5, and\\n[Schoolhouse Press](http://www.schoolhousepress.com/)\\nfor granting us permission to use two photos (patterns 4 and 6)\\nfrom [A Fourth Treasury of Knitting Patterns by Barbara G. Walker](http://www.amazon.com/A-Fourth-Treasury-Knitting-Patterns/dp/0942018206).\\n\\n## Videos\\n\\nStitch Meshes for Modeling Knitted Clothing with Yarn-level Detail (SIGGRAPH 2012) - YouTube\\n\\nTap to unmute\\n\\n[Stitch Meshes for Modeling Knitted Clothing with Yarn-level Detail (SIGGRAPH 2012)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NG5C_a6rxrY) [Cem Yuksel](https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCfGIWnnZY79KO0b12O57byw)\\n\\nCem Yuksel23.6K subscribers\\n\\n[Watch on](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NG5C_a6rxrY)\\n\\nStitch Meshes for Modeling Knitted Clothing with Yarn-level Detail, SIGGRAPH 2012 paper video.\\n\\n## Publications\\n\\n[Yura Hwang](http://yurahwang.com/), [Jenny Han Lin](https://jlin98.github.io/), [Jerry Hsu](https://chichenghsu.com/), [Benjamin Mastripolito](https://www.linkedin.com/in/benjamin-mastripolito-291a1316a/), [Jim McCann](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~jmccann/), [Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/)[Neighbor-Aware Data-Driven Relaxation of Stitch Mesh Models for Knits](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/neighbor-aware-stitch-mesh-relaxation.pdf)In SIGGRAPH Asia 2025 Conference Papers\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/neighbor-aware-stitch-mesh-relaxation-paper.jpg)](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/neighbor-aware-stitch-mesh-relaxation.pdf)\\n\\n[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-pdf.svg)Paper](https://www.cemyuksel.com/research/stitchmeshes/neighbor-aware-stitch-mesh-relaxation.pdf) \\\\- 41.3MB[![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-library.svg)Publisher's Page](https://doi.org/10.1145/3757377.3763890)![](https://www.cemyuksel.com/images/icon-bibtex.svg)BibTeX\\n\\n[Kui Wu](https://kuiwuchn.github.io/), [Marco Tarini](http://vcg.isti.cnr.it/~tarini/), [Cem Yuksel](http://www.cemyuksel.com/), [Jim McCann](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~jmccann/), [Xifeng Gao](https://gaoxifeng.github.io/)[Wearable 3D Machine Knitti